Little Blue Book 
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius pioneered the concept of cutting down classics, a concept later used by `something`.

If a book sold less than `a certain number of copies` in one year, Emanuel Haldeman-Julius would remove it from his line.

Following World War II, the FBI under `someone` considered the Little Blue Books' inclusion of atheism as a threat. 
Emanuel Haldeman-Julius pioneered the concept of cutting down classics, a concept later used by Reader's Digest.

If a book sold less than 10,000 copies in one year, Emanuel Haldeman-Julius would remove it from his line.

Following World War II, the FBI under J. Edgar Hoover considered the Little Blue Books' inclusion of atheism as a threat.

